  "body": " Linear Patterns II   Activity: Visual Patterns (Set 2)   For each of the following patterns, complete the following steps:  Draw Case 4 and Case 5.  Complete the table.  Determine whether the growth is linear and explain how you know.  If linear, find an equation for the number of shapes ( ) in terms of the case number ( ).  Check that your equation works by plugging in some cases from the table to see that they match.       Pattern 1    A growing plus-sign shape made of orange squares. Case 1 has a center block with one block extending up, down, left, and right (5 total). Each subsequent case adds one block to each of the four arms.   Three plus-sign figures made of orange squares for Cases 1, 2, and 3, followed by two dashed empty boxes for students to draw Cases 4 and 5.        Complete the table.    Case ( )  1 2 3 4 5    Shapes  5 9 13        Is the growth linear ? Circle one: Yes \/ No . Explain how you know.      If linear, write an equation for the number of shapes in terms of the case number . Then check your equation using at least two values from the table.  Equation:       Pattern 2    A growing staircase of stacked columns of red squares. Case 1 has a single column of 2 squares. Case 2 adds a taller column of 3 squares to the right (5 total). Case 3 adds another column of 4 squares to the right (9 total). Each new case adds a column one block taller than the previous one.   Three staircase arrangements of red squares for Cases 1, 2, and 3, followed by two dashed empty boxes for students to draw Cases 4 and 5.        Complete the table.    Case ( )  1 2 3 4 5    Shapes  2 5 9        Is the growth linear ? Circle one: Yes \/ No . Explain how you know.      If linear, write an equation for the number of shapes in terms of the case number . Then check your equation using at least two values from the table.  Equation:         Pattern 3    A growing T-shape made of teal squares. Case 1 has a row of 3 squares across the top with 1 square below the center (4 total). Each subsequent case adds one square to each end of the top row and one square below the stem.   Three T-shaped block figures made of teal squares for Cases 1, 2, and 3, followed by two dashed empty boxes for students to draw Cases 4 and 5.        Complete the table.    Case ( )  1 2 3 4 5    Shapes  4 7 10        Is the growth linear ? Circle one: Yes \/ No . Explain how you know.      If linear, write an equation for the number of shapes in terms of the case number . Then check your equation using at least two values from the table.  Equation:       Pattern 4    A growing arrangement of purple squares arranged in columns of 5. Case 1 has one column of 5 squares. Each subsequent case adds one more full column of 5 squares to the right, with a small gap between columns to make each addition clear.   Three arrangements of purple squares stacked in columns of 5 for Cases 1, 2, and 3, followed by two dashed empty boxes for students to draw Cases 4 and 5.        Complete the table.    Case ( )  1 2 3 4 5    Shapes  5 10 15        Is the growth linear ? Circle one: Yes \/ No . Explain how you know.      If linear, write an equation for the number of shapes in terms of the case number . Then check your equation using at least two values from the table.  Equation:         Is It Linear? Tables Only   For each table below, decide whether the relationship shown is linear or not linear . Explain how you know. If it is linear, write an equation for in terms of .        0 1 2 3 4     3 4.5 6 7.5 9    Linear or not linear? Explain. If linear, write an equation.    Linear. The -values increase by each time increases by , so the rate of change is constant. The vertical intercept is , giving the equation .         0 1 2 3 4     20 17.5 15 12.5 10    Linear or not linear? Explain. If linear, write an equation.    Linear. The -values decrease by each time increases by , so the rate of change is constant (and negative). The vertical intercept is , giving the equation .         1 2 3 4 5     1 2 6 24 120    Linear or not linear? Explain. If linear, write an equation.    Not linear. The differences between consecutive -values are -- these are not constant, so the rate of change is not constant. In fact each -value is the factorial of : , , , , . The growth is far too rapid to be linear.         1 2 3 4 5     4 9 11 18 20    Linear or not linear? Explain. If linear, write an equation.    Not linear. The differences between consecutive -values are -- these are not constant, so the relationship is not linear. There is no single steady rate of change.       "
