  "title": "Cricket Chirps and Temperature.",
  "body": " Cricket Chirps and Temperature   Crickets are ectotherms, meaning their body temperature (and their chirping speed) is driven by the temperature around them. The table below shows real field data: the number of chirps a striped ground cricket made in a 15-second span, along with the air temperature at the time. Data source: G. W. Pierce, The Song of Insects , Harvard University Press.       Chirps (in 15 seconds)  Temperature (degrees Fahrenheit)   20 89  16 72  20 93  18 84  17 81  16 75  15 70  17 82  15 69  16 83  15 80  17 83  16 81  17 84  14 76      Enter the data into a table in Desmos and adjust your window. Does there appear to be an approximately linear structure?      Find the linear regression model for this data.     Enter the data. Input the table of values into Desmos, with chirps as and temperature as .   Find the regression line. Click the style\/regression icon next to the table, or type in the command line below your data matrix.   View high precision parameters. Type or into an empty evaluation line underneath to view extended decimal precision values.    Regression equation: (round the parameters to 3 decimal places)      Look at your graphed equation along with the data points. Does there seem to be a relationship between the number of chirps and the air temperature?      A cricket chirps 19 times in a 15-second span. What temperature would you expect it to be?      Suppose the air temperature is F. How many chirps would you expect to hear in 15 seconds?      What is the slope of your model? Write one sentence to describe what it means in the context of this problem.      What is the vertical intercept of your model? Write one sentence to describe what it means in the context of this problem. Does this value make sense here?      What is the horizontal intercept of your model? Write one sentence to describe what it means in the context of this problem. Does this value make sense here?    "
