  "title": "Learning Target 5: Linear Models",
  "body": " Learning Target 5: Linear Models    Given a linear word situation, make an equation, identify and explain the meaning of slope in the context, identify and explain the y-intercept in the context, find and explain its meaning, find given and explain its meaning, and determine what inputs make sense.       Dining Dollars   Professor Hallas starts the semester with dining dollars on a campus meal account. Each visit to the cafe costs .     Pick variables to describe this situation, and indicate what each variable represents (including units).        Variable (letter)  Meaning of the variable (including units)    Independent      Dependent              Variable (letter)  Meaning of the variable (including units)    Independent   Number of visits to the cafe    Dependent   Dining dollars remaining (dollars)       What is the slope of your model, and what does it mean in the context of this situation?  The slope is . It means that with each visit to the cafe, the balance decreases by .    What is the y-intercept of your model, and what does it mean in the context of this situation?  The y-intercept is . It means that before any visits, the account started with dining dollars.    Write an equation to model the dining dollars remaining. Show your thinking. Be careful which variables you use.   Using with the variables and , substitute the slope for and the starting value for : .     According to your model, how many dining dollars remain after visits? (That is, find .) Show your thinking.   Substitute : . After visits, dining dollars remain.     After how many visits will dining dollars remain? (That is, find so that .) Show your thinking.   Set and solve: , so and . After visits, dining dollars remain.     What inputs make sense in this situation? Explain.   The input is a whole number of visits, , and the balance cannot go negative. After visits only remains, which is not enough for a th visit, so the inputs that make sense are the whole numbers from to .         Healing in Overwatch   In the game Overwatch , the support hero Mercy heals a teammate at a steady rate (assume no other effects, such as passive regeneration or healing reduction). A low-health teammate is being healed: after seconds they are at HP, and after seconds they are at HP. The teammate's maximum health is HP.     Pick variables to describe this situation, and indicate what each variable represents (including units).        Variable (letter)  Meaning of the variable (including units)    Independent      Dependent              Variable (letter)  Meaning of the variable (including units)    Independent   Time since healing began (seconds)    Dependent   Teammate's health (HP)       Write a linear equation to model the teammate's health. Show your thinking.   Treat the data as two points, and .  Slope: .  Substitute into : , so .  Final equation: .     What is the slope of your model, and what does it mean in the context of this situation?  The slope is . It means Mercy restores HP each second.    What is the y-intercept of your model, and what does it mean in the context of this situation?  The y-intercept is . It means the teammate had HP at the moment healing began.    According to your model, how much health does the teammate have after seconds? (That is, find .) Show your thinking.   Substitute : . After seconds, the teammate has HP.     According to your model, how long until the teammate reaches full health ( HP)? Show your thinking.   Set and solve: , so and seconds. It takes about seconds to reach full health.     What inputs make sense in this situation? Explain.   Time cannot be negative, so . Also, health cannot exceed the maximum of HP, so the linear model only applies until the teammate is fully healed, at about seconds. The inputs that make sense are roughly seconds.         Gym Membership   A gym membership has a one-time registration fee plus a fixed monthly charge. The registration fee is , and the membership costs per month.     Pick variables to describe this situation, and indicate what each variable represents (including units).        Variable (letter)  Meaning of the variable (including units)    Independent      Dependent              Variable (letter)  Meaning of the variable (including units)    Independent   Number of months of membership    Dependent   Total amount paid (dollars)       What is the slope of your model, and what does it mean in the context of this situation?  The slope is . It means the total cost increases by for each additional month of membership.    What is the y-intercept of your model, and what does it mean in the context of this situation?  The y-intercept is . It is the registration fee, paid at the start before any monthly charges (at months).    Write an equation to model the total amount paid. Show your thinking. Be careful which variables you use.   Using with the variables and , substitute the slope and the registration fee as the y-intercept: .     According to your model, how much will you have paid in total after months? (That is, find .) Show your thinking.   Substitute : . After months, you will have paid .     After how many months will the total amount paid reach ? (That is, find so that .) Show your thinking.   Set and solve: , so and . After months, the total paid reaches .     What inputs make sense in this situation? Explain.   The input is a whole number of months, ; a negative number of months has no meaning. There is no fixed upper limit, since the membership can continue, so the inputs that make sense are the whole numbers .         Estimating Maximum Heart Rate   Exercise scientists estimate a person's maximum heart rate from their age, and the relationship is linear. Two estimates are given: a -year-old has an estimated maximum heart rate of bpm, and a -year-old has an estimated maximum heart rate of bpm.     Pick variables to describe this situation, and indicate what each variable represents (including units).        Variable (letter)  Meaning of the variable (including units)    Independent      Dependent              Variable (letter)  Meaning of the variable (including units)    Independent   Age (years)    Dependent   Estimated maximum heart rate (bpm)       Write a linear equation to model the estimated maximum heart rate. Show your thinking.   Treat the data as two points, and .  Slope: .  Substitute into : , so .  Final equation: . (This is the Tanaka formula, , used by exercise physiologists.)     What is the slope of your model, and what does it mean in the context of this situation?  The slope is . It means the estimated maximum heart rate decreases by bpm for each additional year of age.    What is the y-intercept of your model, and what does it mean in the context of this situation?   The y-intercept is , the model's value at age . A newborn is not a meaningful input for this formula, so does not have a realistic interpretation here—it is the mathematical y-intercept rather than a true maximum heart rate at birth.     Use your model to estimate the maximum heart rate of a -year-old. (That is, find .) Show your thinking.   Substitute : . The estimated maximum heart rate of a -year-old is bpm.     For what age does the model predict a maximum heart rate of bpm? (That is, find so that .) Show your thinking.   Set and solve: , so and . The model predicts a maximum heart rate of bpm at age .     What inputs make sense in this situation? Explain.   The input is a person's age, so should be positive, and the formula is intended for human ages—roughly the teens through about to years. Age , negative ages, and very large ages do not give meaningful estimates.      "
