  "body": " Situation A: Softball Launcher   A pitching machine on the roof of a parking ramp is aimed straight up and fires a softball. The height of the softball in feet above the ground, seconds after it is fired, is given by   The graph of is shown below. The horizontal intercepts, vertical intercept, and vertex all occur at integer coordinates.   A downward opening parabola showing height in feet against time in seconds, with horizontal intercepts at negative one and five, vertical intercept at eighty, and vertex at two comma one hundred forty-four.   A parabola opening downward on a coordinate grid with time on the horizontal axis and height on the vertical axis. The curve crosses the horizontal axis at the points negative one comma zero and five comma zero, crosses the vertical axis at zero comma eighty, and reaches its highest point at two comma one hundred forty-four.        Write in factored form and in vertex form. Show the work that produces each one.    For factored form, pull out the common factor of first: . Two numbers that multiply to and add to are and , so .  For vertex form, the vertex is halfway between the two zeros and , so . (Using gives the same thing.) Then , so . Check at : , which matches the vertical intercept.      Identify the vertical intercept of the graph. What does it mean in this situation?    The vertical intercept is . It is the height at time , which is the instant the softball leaves the machine. So the softball starts feet above the ground, meaning the pitching machine sits on top of an -foot parking ramp. It is also the constant term of the general form, .      Identify the horizontal intercepts of the graph. Explain what each one means, and say which one actually describes the softball.    The horizontal intercepts are and . A horizontal intercept is a time when the height is feet, which means the softball is at ground level.  The intercept is the meaningful one: the softball lands on the ground seconds after it is fired. The intercept would be one second before the machine fired, so it does not describe anything that happens in this situation. It is a real solution of the equation but not a real moment in the story.      Identify the vertex. What does it mean in this situation?    The vertex is . Since the parabola opens downward, this is the highest point on the graph. It means the softball reaches its maximum height of feet above the ground seconds after it is fired. Both coordinates matter here: the answers \"when\" and the answers \"how high.\"      Find . What does this value tell you about the softball?    Using factored form, feet. (General form agrees: .)  Four seconds after being fired, the softball is feet above the ground. Since is past the vertex at , the ball is on its way down. Notice this is the same height it started at, which makes sense: and are the same distance from the vertex, so the parabola gives them the same output.      Find all values of for which . Show your algebraic steps and explain what your answers mean.    Set the general form equal to : . Subtract from both sides to get . Divide every term by to get , which factors as . The solutions are and .  The softball is feet above the ground twice: once at second on the way up, and again at seconds on the way down. Both times are meaningful here since both fall between launch and landing. The two answers are symmetric about the vertex at , which is exactly what a horizontal line crossing a parabola twice should give.      What values of make sense as inputs in this situation? Explain how you decided.    The inputs that make sense are .  Negative values of would be before the machine fired, and the model does not describe the softball then. Values larger than would be after the ball has already landed, and the function returns negative heights there, which would mean the ball is underground. For example, , which is not a possible height. So even though the algebra will hand you outputs for every real number, only the times from the launch through the landing describe this softball.    "
